---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
const chapters = [
  ["01", "Getting started", "Run RustyAuth and SableDB locally with Docker Compose.", "/docs/getting-started"],
  ["02", "Architecture", "Understand trust boundaries, durable state and authentication flows.", "/docs/architecture"],
  ["03", "Identity data model", "See every persisted identity field, invariant and API projection.", "/docs/identity-data"],
  ["04", "HTTP API", "Review public endpoints, access requirements and response contracts.", "/docs/api"],
  ["05", "Configuration", "Configure origins, tokens, sessions, persistence and backups.", "/docs/configuration"],
  ["06", "Backups and key rotation", "Operate, rotate, verify and rehearse clean-room recovery.", "/docs/recovery"],
  ["07", "Deployment", "Package RustyAuth safely for Docker and Railway.", "/docs/deployment"],
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Own the identity boundary." eyebrow="RustyAuth documentation" intro="Everything you need to understand, run and integrate the pre-release RustyAuth service.">
  <div class="doc-callout warning"><strong>Pre-release</strong><p>Account recovery, abuse controls, multi-writer qualification and an independent security assessment are not complete.</p></div>
  <h2>Start here</h2>
  <div class="docs-card-grid">{chapters.map(([number, title, body, href]) => <a class="docs-card" href={href}><small>{number}</small><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p><span>Read chapter →</span></a>)}</div>
  <h2>What RustyAuth does</h2>
  <p>RustyAuth authenticates an identity, maintains revocable browser sessions and issues short-lived claims for downstream services. It intentionally leaves roles, permissions, entitlements and resource ownership to the application.</p>
</DocsLayout>
